$post_body = <<<'HTML'
<p>Homeowners with nonstandard window sizes, high ceilings, or a specific light blocking need are usually better served by custom draperies, while ready-made panels work fine for standard windows where an exact fit is not critical. The real difference comes down to measurement: custom panels are sewn for one window, and ready-made panels are sewn to a handful of standard lengths and widths. <a href="%BASE%/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Creative Blinds and Drapes</a> helps homeowners in the Aurora area figure out which option actually fits their windows before they spend money on either one.</p>
%IMAGE1%
<h2 id="who-benefits-most-from-custom-draperies">Who Benefits Most From Custom Draperies?</h2>
<p>Custom panels earn their price when a window does not match standard retail sizes, or when the room has a specific job to do, like blocking morning light in a bedroom. <a href="%BASE%/window-treatments/curtains-and-drapes/draperies/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Custom draperies</a> are measured and sewn to the window, so fullness, length, and lining can all be set exactly instead of guessed.</p>
<p>Custom draperies are usually the better fit for:</p>
<ul class="bp-list">
  <li>Windows taller than 84 inches or wider than standard retail sizes</li>
  <li>Bay windows, arched windows, or windows close to a corner</li>
  <li>Bedrooms or media rooms that need real light blocking</li>
  <li>Homeowners who want a specific fabric, pattern, or lining combination</li>
  <li>Formal rooms where fit and fall of the fabric matter</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="who-can-get-by-with-ready-made-panels">Who Can Get By With Ready-Made Panels?</h2>
<p>Ready-made panels make sense for a standard sized window where a close enough fit is acceptable, or for a room being refreshed on a smaller budget. They come in a handful of set lengths, usually 63, 84, 96, and 108 inches, which fits many but not all windows. Our <a href="%BASE%/window-treatments/curtains-and-drapes/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">curtains and drapes</a> overview explains where ready-made and custom options both fit into a full window treatment plan.</p>
<p>Ready-made panels tend to work for:</p>
<ul class="bp-list">
  <li>Standard windows in rentals or starter homes</li>
  <li>Secondary rooms like guest bedrooms or home offices</li>
  <li>Homeowners testing a color or style before committing to custom</li>
  <li>Quick updates where exact length is not critical</li>
</ul>
%IMAGE2%
<h2 id="what-should-guide-the-decision">What Should Guide the Decision?</h2>
<p>The decision usually comes down to how particular the fit needs to be and how long the homeowner plans to stay in the home. A short term renter rarely needs custom panels, while a homeowner doing a full room renovation often does.</p>
<p>Questions worth asking before choosing:</p>
<ul class="bp-list">
  <li>Is the window a standard size, or noticeably taller, wider, or narrower?</li>
  <li>Does the room need real light blocking, or just a decorative layer?</li>
  <li>How long will these panels stay in this specific window?</li>
  <li>Is an exact fabric match to existing decor part of the goal?</li>
</ul>
<p>Homeowners weighing cost against fit may also want to read our guide on <a href="%BASE%/blog/what-is-cheaper-than-blinds/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">what is cheaper than blinds</a> for a broader cost comparison.</p>
<p>Neither option is wrong on its own, and the right choice depends on the window itself and how the room gets used. If you are not sure which category your windows fall into, <a href="%BASE%/contact/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">schedule a free in-home consultation</a> and a member of the team can measure and advise on the spot.</p>
HTML;

